  "name": null,
  "problem": "Robert bought $3$ pounds of walnuts while Susan bought $\\frac{17}{9}$ of a pound of walnuts. How many walnuts did they buy together in total? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        They bought$\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$pounds of walnuts in total.",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the number of walnuts Robert and Susan bought together in total, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{17}{9}\n$$\n\nTo add $3$ and $\\frac{17}{9},$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $9.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $9$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{1\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{9}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{9}+\\frac{17}{9}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{9}+\\frac{17}{9}=\\frac{27+17}{9}=\\frac{44}{9}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n44÷9=4\\,\\text{R}\\,8=4\\,\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, they bought $4\\,\\frac{8}{9}$ pounds of walnuts.",
